<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lands of the Bohemian Crown</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1348">1348</a>–<a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Crown of Bohemia, Bohemian Crown</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lands of the Bohemian Crown were the territories united under the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>: the Kingdom of Bohemia, the Margraviate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, Silesia and, for long periods, Upper and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-lusatia">Lower Lusatia</a>. Charles IV defined them as a single union under the Crown of Bohemia in 1348. Ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> from 1526, they later passed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a>, and Bohemia, Moravia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-silesia">Czech Silesia</a> form today's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lands-of-the-bohemian-crown.jpg" alt="Lands of the Bohemian Crown" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Urmas · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Bohemia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In the tenth and eleventh centuries the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> and Kłodzko Land were consolidated under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/premyslid-dynasty">Přemyslid dynasty</a>. Duke Ottokar I gained the hereditary royal title in 1198 from the German king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-of-swabia">Philip of Swabia</a>, and Frederick II confirmed it in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-sicily">Golden Bull of Sicily</a> of 1212.</p><p>Ottokar II acquired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a> between 1251 and 1269, but Rudolph I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> seized them and defeated him in 1278. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/premyslid-dynasty">Přemyslids</a> died out in 1306 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-luxembourg">House of Luxembourg</a> produced the kings. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-bohemia">John the Blind</a> made most of the Piast dukes of Silesia his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, and the Polish king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iii-the-great">Casimir III the Great</a> acknowledged his suzerainty in 1335.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Charles IV, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> from 1346 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a> from 1355, established the joint rule of the Corona regni Bohemiae by decree on 7 April 1348, tying the lands to the Bohemian monarchy and not to a king or dynasty. He decreed that they should endure even if the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourgs</a> died out, and in 1367 he bought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-lusatia">Lower Lusatia</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1356">Golden Bull of 1356</a> made the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> a prince-elector. The union outlived the Hussite Wars and the extinction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> male line in 1437. Vladislas II, a Jagiellon, became king in 1471, and in 1479 the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olomouc">Olomouc</a> kept the lands united while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthias-corvinus">Matthias Corvinus</a> held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, Silesia and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatias</a>.</p><p>After Louis was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mohacs">Battle of Mohács</a> in 1526, Bohemian nobles elected the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> archduke Ferdinand I as king. Attempts by Protestant estates to build an autonomous confederation ended at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-white-mountain">Battle of White Mountain</a> in 1620, and administration was centralised at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatias</a> passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> in 1635, and most of Silesia to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-breslau">Treaty of Breslau</a> of 1742.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Crown was neither a personal union nor a federation of equal members: the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> had a higher status than the other lands. Only a few common institutions existed, and they did not survive the centralisation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a>. The most important, the Bohemian Court Chancellery, was united with the Austrian Chancellery in 1749.</p><p>The remaining lands became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a> in 1804 and of the Cisleithanian half of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> in 1867. After 1918 they were the Czech lands of the First Czechoslovak Republic, and Austrian Silesia is now known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-silesia">Czech Silesia</a>, apart from eastern Cieszyn Silesia, which passed to the Second Polish Republic in 1920.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lands of the Bohemian Crown” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…I. The title gave a common name to the composite monarchy the Habsburgs had built since the sixteenth century: the Austrian hereditary lands, the <b>Lands of the Bohemian Crown</b>, the Kingdom of Hungary with Croatia, the Grand Principality of Transylvania, and Galicia, taken in the partitions of Poland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom stood at the centre of the <b>Lands of the Bohemian Crown</b>, a union that Charles IV formalised in 1348 and which included Moravia, the Silesian duchies, most of them made Bohemian fiefs by King John of Luxembourg between 1327 and 1335, and Upper and Lower Lusatia;…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Charles IV, margrave from 1334, declared Moravia a fief of the <b>Crown of Bohemia</b> in 1348 and in 1349 granted it to his brother John Henry, whose sons Jobst and Prokop fought each other over it for years; Jobst, who also held Brandenburg, was elected King of the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-moravia">Margraviate of Moravia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
